<BaseLayout title="Conveyor Engineering Calculations, Belt Pull, Motor Sizing, Speed &#x26; Throughput | PackagingConveyor.com" description="Conveyor engineering formulas: belt pull, motor HP sizing, speed and throughput, accumulation zone sizing, incline limits, and transfer points." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/resources/conveyor-design-guide/conveyor-engineering/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
<h1>Conveyor Engineering Calculations</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Conveyor engineering calculations turn product data and layout dimensions into belt pull, motor size, belt speed, and throughput. The core formulas are belt pull (effective tension), drive horsepower HP = (Te × V) / 33,000, throughput = belt speed / product pitch, and accumulation length = buffer time × rate × pitch. Use them to check supplier proposals and to catch undersized drives early. Final sizing should use the friction coefficients and efficiencies published by the belt and gearmotor makers for your exact components. These formulas apply to belt conveyors, roller conveyors, and most other <a href="/conveyor-types/">conveyor types</a> used in packaging lines.</p>
<h2 id="beltpull-heading">Belt Pull Calculations</h2>
<p>Belt pull, also called effective tension (Te), is the force the drive pulley must apply to the belt to keep the loaded conveyor moving at constant speed. It sets motor power, belt tensile rating, and shaft and bearing loads. Every drive sizing starts here.</p>
<h3>Slider Bed Belt Pull Formula</h3>
<p>On a slider bed conveyor the carrying side of the belt slides on a flat bed and the return side runs on rollers. Forbo Siegling's <a href="https://www.forbo.com/media/document/4ebaace0-008f-466a-b319-6313530e12d8" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Transilon calculation method</a> treats these two parts separately. Written in pounds-force (the g term drops out), it is:</p>
<p class="formula">Te = μ<sub>T</sub> × (P + B/2) + μ<sub>R</sub> × (B/2 + R) + P × sin α</p>
<p>Where: <strong>Te</strong> = effective tension (belt pull), lb. <strong>P</strong> = total weight of product on the carrying side, lb. <strong>B</strong> = weight of the whole belt (carry and return runs), lb. <strong>R</strong> = weight of the return rollers and all pulleys except the drive pulley, lb. <strong>μ<sub>T</sub></strong> = friction coefficient between belt underside and slider bed. <strong>μ<sub>R</sub></strong> = friction coefficient for running over rollers. <strong>α</strong> = incline angle. Use minus instead of plus on the last term for a decline. P × sin α equals the product weight per foot of belt times the elevation change in feet.</p>
<p>The first term is sliding friction on the bed. The second is rolling resistance on the return side. The third lifts the product. On a horizontal conveyor, α = 0 and the third term drops out.</p>
<h3>Friction Coefficients</h3>
<p>The friction coefficient drives the answer more than any other input, and published values differ by belt type and by calculation method. Do not mix a coefficient from one maker's method with another maker's formula.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Source</th><th>Condition</th><th>Published value</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Forbo Siegling, Transilon calculation method</td><td>Fabric belt underside on slider bed (μ<sub>T</sub>)</td><td>0.33</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forbo Siegling, Transilon calculation method</td><td>Coated undersides such as U1, V1, VH (μ<sub>T</sub>)</td><td>0.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forbo Siegling, Transilon calculation method</td><td>Belt running over rollers (μ<sub>R</sub>)</td><td>0.033</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sparks Belting, conveyor belt calculations</td><td>Slider bed, bare duck to friction surface</td><td>0.20 to 0.55</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forbo Siegling, Prolink modular belt guide</td><td>Plastic modular belt on wearstrips, by material and cleanliness</td><td>About 0.08 to 0.59</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Siegling notes that its fabric belt values reflect worn, soiled surfaces and are about 1.5 times higher than values for new surfaces. <a href="https://www.sparksbelting.com/blog/understanding-conveyor-belt-calculations" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sparks Belting's simplified method</a> uses E = F × (P + M), with the full belt weight M in the friction term, and lists its own roller bed factors. For modular plastic belts, the <a href="https://www.forbo.com/media/document/8d828b24-c03a-498c-ab3b-713371a7a5d5" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Siegling Prolink guide</a> tables coefficients by belt material (PE, PP, POM, PA), wearstrip material (UHMW PE, HDPE, steel, and others), and clean, regular, or soiled conditions. In every row of that table, soiled values are well above clean values.</p>
<h3>Roller Bed Belt Pull Formula</h3>
<p>On a roller bed belt conveyor, both runs ride on rollers. Siegling's formula becomes:</p>
<p class="formula">Te = μ<sub>R</sub> × (P + B + R) + P × sin α</p>
<p>With μ<sub>R</sub> = 0.033 in Siegling's method against 0.33 for a fabric belt on a slider bed, the friction part of belt pull is roughly a tenth for the same load. That is why roller bed construction is used for long runs and heavy loads.</p>
<h3>Accumulation on a Running Belt</h3>
<p>If product stops against a blade stop or a downstream machine while the belt keeps running, the belt slides under the product. Siegling adds a third friction term, μ<sub>ST</sub> × P, where μ<sub>ST</sub> is the belt-to-product coefficient (0.33 or 0.5 in Siegling's table, depending on belt surface). Accumulation can add a large share of belt pull, so state it in the RFQ.</p>
<h3>Belt Pull Example</h3>
<p>A 50-foot horizontal slider bed conveyor carries 30-lb cases spaced 2 feet apart, so 25 cases are on the belt: P = 750 lb. The belt weighs 1.0 lb per foot and is about 100 feet long: B = 100 lb. Return rollers and the tail pulley weigh R = 60 lb. Use μ<sub>T</sub> = 0.33 and μ<sub>R</sub> = 0.033 (Siegling guideline values). These are example values. Get the actual coefficients and weights from the belt maker.</p>
<p class="formula">Te = 0.33 × (750 + 50) + 0.033 × (50 + 60) + 0<br>
Te = 264 + 3.6 = 267.6 lb (about 268 lb)</p>
<p>The friction term on the slider bed dominates. Belt pull does not depend on speed at steady state. That is why <a href="https://literature.rockwellautomation.com/idc/groups/literature/documents/wp/drives-wp008_-en-p.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rockwell Automation classifies conveyors as constant torque loads</a>. Speed enters in the next step.</p>
<h2 id="motor-heading">Motor Sizing</h2>
<p>Power is force times velocity. In U.S. units, 1 HP = 33,000 ft·lbf/min, which gives the standard formula used in <a href="https://www.sparksbelting.com/blog/understanding-conveyor-belt-calculations" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">belt makers' calculation guides</a>.</p>
<h3>Horsepower Formula</h3>
<p class="formula">HP<sub>drive</sub> = (Te × V) / 33,000<br>
HP<sub>motor</sub> = HP<sub>drive</sub> / η</p>
<p>Where: <strong>Te</strong> = belt pull, lb. <strong>V</strong> = belt speed, ft/min (FPM). <strong>η</strong> = efficiency of the drive train between motor and pulley (gear reducer, chain, couplings). Siegling's method calculates power at the drive drum and divides by drive efficiency before selecting the next larger standard motor. Gear reducer efficiency varies widely by reducer type and ratio, so take η from the reducer maker's catalog.</p>
<h3>Service Factor and Design Margin</h3>
<p>The formula gives steady-state power. Real conveyors also start under load, accelerate product, and see surges. Suppliers handle this with a design margin. <a href="https://www.sparksbelting.com/cd/calculator_sliderbed.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sparks Belting's slider bed HP calculator</a> uses a default service factor of 1.50 and directs users to a chart for adjustments. Gear reducer makers publish separate service factors for the reducer itself, based on load type, starts per hour, and hours per day. Check both. Frequent starts, indexing, reversing, and high-inertia loads call for more margin than a continuous, evenly loaded run.</p>
<p>Round up to the next standard motor rating. Standard NEMA ratings in the <a href="https://www.casa.co.nz/motors/NMA-MG1-Condensed-67p.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">condensed NEMA MG 1 guide</a> include 1/4 and 1/3 HP for small motors and 1/2, 3/4, 1, 1-1/2, 2, 3, 5, 7-1/2, and 10 HP for polyphase medium motors. An undersized motor overheats and trips. A grossly oversized motor runs at poor efficiency. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">motors and drives guide</a> covers motor types, efficiency classes, and VFD selection.</p>
<h3>Motor Sizing Example</h3>
<p>Continuing the example: Te = 267.6 lb, belt speed 100 FPM, drive efficiency η = 0.85, service factor 1.5. The efficiency and service factor are example values. Use the figures your gearmotor and belt suppliers give you.</p>
<p class="formula">HP<sub>drive</sub> = (267.6 × 100) / 33,000 = 0.81 HP<br>
HP<sub>motor</sub> = 0.81 / 0.85 = 0.95 HP<br>
With service factor: 0.95 × 1.5 = 1.43 HP<br>
Next standard size = 1.5 HP</p>
<p>Note how much the friction coefficient matters. A coefficient several times too low would have pointed to a fractional motor that could not start this conveyor loaded. Ask suppliers which coefficient and efficiency they used.</p>
<BeltPullCalculator id="belt-pull-calculator" />
<h2 id="speed-heading">Speed Calculations</h2>
<p>Belt speed sets throughput and is measured in feet per minute (FPM). It depends on drive pulley diameter and pulley RPM.</p>
<h3>Belt Speed Formula</h3>
<p class="formula">V = π × D × RPM / 12</p>
<p>Where: <strong>V</strong> = belt speed, FPM. <strong>D</strong> = drive pulley diameter, inches (include lagging if fitted). <strong>RPM</strong> = drive pulley speed (motor RPM / gear ratio). Dividing by 12 converts inches to feet.</p>
<p>A 4-inch drive pulley at 95 RPM gives V = 3.1416 × 4 × 95 / 12 = 99.5 FPM. To change speed, change the gear ratio, change the pulley diameter, or change motor speed with a VFD. A VFD is the easiest: the operator sets speed at the HMI or the PLC sets it automatically.</p>
<h3>Choosing a Line Speed</h3>
<p>Packaging conveyor speed is set by the rate and pitch the line needs, not by a standard figure. Manual pack stations run slow enough for workers to handle product. Case conveying runs faster. Container lines often run fastest. Conveyors feeding a machine are usually set somewhat faster than the machine's infeed demand so the machine is not starved. The machine builder's infeed spec is the number to design to.</p>
<h2 id="throughput-heading">Throughput Calculations</h2>
<p>Throughput is the number of products delivered per unit of time. It ties belt speed, product length, and spacing together.</p>
<h3>Products Per Minute Formula</h3>
<p class="formula">Products/min = V / P</p>
<p>Where: <strong>V</strong> = belt speed, FPM. <strong>P</strong> = product pitch in feet (product length + gap). Touching products have zero gap. Photoeye-based zone control needs a gap large enough for the sensor to see between products. That gap depends on the sensor and product, so confirm it with the controls supplier.</p>
<p>Example: belt speed 80 FPM, product length 10 inches, gap 2 inches. Pitch = 12 inches = 1 foot. Throughput = 80 / 1 = 80 products per minute.</p>
<p>For multi-lane conveyors, multiply single-lane throughput by the number of lanes. Three lanes at 80 per minute deliver 240 per minute. Lane dividers and downstream merges must handle the combined rate.</p>
<h3>Throughput Limitations</h3>
<p>The formula assumes perfect spacing and no stops. Sustained output is lower because of jams, changeovers, and starved or blocked machines. Size conveyors for the required output divided by the line efficiency you actually measure, for example from your own OEE records, rather than for nameplate rate.</p>
<h2 id="accumulation-heading">Accumulation Zone Sizing</h2>
<p>Accumulation conveyors buffer product between machines with different speeds or different stop events. Too little accumulation stops the upstream machine. Too much wastes floor space and capital.</p>
<h3>Accumulation Length Formula</h3>
<p class="formula">L<sub>acc</sub> = T<sub>buffer</sub> × R × P</p>
<p>Where: <strong>L<sub>acc</sub></strong> = accumulation length, ft. <strong>T<sub>buffer</sub></strong> = desired buffer time, minutes. <strong>R</strong> = upstream machine rate, products per minute. <strong>P</strong> = product pitch when accumulated, ft.</p>
<p>Example: upstream rate 60 cases per minute, 18-inch cases accumulated touching (pitch 1.5 ft), buffer 2 minutes. L<sub>acc</sub> = 2 × 60 × 1.5 = 180 feet. That is long. Multi-lane accumulation, spiral accumulators, or bi-directional accumulation tables reduce the footprint. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/">design guide hub</a> covers layout strategies.</p>
<h3>Setting Buffer Time</h3>
<p>Set buffer time from the downstream stops you want the upstream machine to ride through. Pull typical jam-clear and minor-stop durations from your downtime records. If a planned changeover lasts longer than any practical buffer, it is usually simpler to stop the upstream machine for the changeover than to build that much accumulation.</p>
<h2 id="incline-heading">Incline Angle Limits</h2>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">Incline conveyors</a> move product between elevations. The maximum angle depends on friction between product and belt, the product's center of gravity, and the belt surface. Past that angle, product slides back or tips.</p>
<h3>Angle Limits by Belt Surface</h3>
<p>For plastic modular belts, Forbo's Siegling Prolink guide gives these rough guidelines and recommends testing for the actual product:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Belt surface</th><th>Rough achievable incline (Siegling Prolink)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Flat top surface</td><td>3 to 5 degrees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Friction top surface</td><td>20 to 40 degrees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Straight profiles (cleats)</td><td>Under 60 degrees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bent profiles</td><td>Under 90 degrees</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Fabric belts follow the same pattern. A smooth top allows the lowest angle, a rough or friction top allows more, and cleats hold product mechanically at steeper angles. Corrugated sidewall belts with cleats can run near vertical for loose product. For <a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">flat belt conveyors</a> on inclines, a rough-top or friction-top belt is the usual choice. Ask the belt maker for the rated incline for your product and belt surface.</p>
<h3>Product Stability on Inclines</h3>
<p>Tall, narrow products (bottles, jars, cans) have a high center of gravity relative to base width and tip at lower angles than flat cases. Upright bottles on an incline need side guides and a shallow angle, or the product is laid down and carried in a trough. Round products roll back unless contained by cleats, lanes, or cradle belts. Test before fixing the angle.</p>
<h3>Transition Zones</h3>
<p>Transitions from horizontal to incline and back need a curve gentle enough that the product keeps full belt contact. A sharp transition lifts the leading edge and can tip the product. Belt makers publish minimum radii for their belts and profile types. Size the transition for the product as well as the belt, and confirm with a product trial.</p>
<h2 id="transfer-heading">Transfer Point Design</h2>
<p>Transfer points move product between conveyors or between a conveyor and a machine. Each one is a likely jam point and a source of product damage. The three basics are a small gap, matched speeds, and aligned heights.</p>
<h3>Gap and Dead Plate Transfers</h3>
<p>Two belt conveyors placed end to end leave a gap between the discharge pulley and the receiving pulley. Small, flexible, or light products can catch there. A dead plate bridges the gap. It must be smooth, level with both belts, and short enough that the product does not stall on it.</p>
<p>Small-diameter nose bars (knife edges) reduce the gap. Center-drive conveyors allow nose bars at both ends because the drive pulley is underneath. End-drive conveyors have a larger radius at the drive end.</p>
<h3>Speed Matching at Transfers</h3>
<p>Run the receiving conveyor at the same speed as the discharging conveyor or slightly faster. Slightly faster pulls a small gap and keeps product from piling up at the transfer. Slower causes bunching and jams. Set the ratio during commissioning with real product.</p>
<h3>Height Alignment</h3>
<p>Belt surfaces at a transfer must be level with each other. A step catches the product's leading edge. Adjustable legs with leveling feet allow fine adjustment during installation. Ask the supplier what height tolerance their transfers need for your product. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">cost guide</a> notes that installation labor for alignment grows with the number of transfer points.</p>
<h2 id="roller-calc-heading">Roller Conveyor Calculations</h2>
<p>Roller conveyors add three parameters: roller diameter, roller centers (pitch), and load per roller.</p>
<h3>Roller Spacing</h3>
<p><a href="https://www.ashlandconveyor.com/pages/roller-conveyor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ashland Conveyor's roller conveyor guide</a> states that a minimum of three rollers should be in contact with the load at all times. That makes roller pitch no greater than one-third of the shortest product length in the direction of travel. A 12-inch case needs roller centers of 4 inches or less. A 24-inch product needs 8 inches or less. Closer centers also reduce load per roller.</p>
<h3>Roller Load Capacity</h3>
<p>Load per roller equals product weight divided by the number of rollers under it. A 50-lb case on four rollers puts 12.5 lb on each. Use the conservative case for uneven bottoms, which may sit on fewer rollers. Compare the result with the roller maker's published rating for that tube, axle, and bearing, and keep margin.</p>
<h3>Gravity Conveyor Slope</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">Gravity roller conveyors</a> need a slight decline. The right slope depends on product weight, bottom condition, bearing quality, and roller diameter. Light products need more slope. Heavy products need less to avoid runaway. Set the final pitch by testing actual product on the actual conveyor. On long declines, brake rollers or retarders limit product speed.</p>
