<h2 id="faq-heading">Engineering FAQ</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">How do you calculate conveyor belt pull?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>For a slider bed conveyor, Forbo Siegling's method gives Te = μ<sub>T</sub> × (P + B/2) + μ<sub>R</sub> × (B/2 + R) + P × sin α. P is total product weight on the carrying side, B is the weight of the whole belt, R is the weight of return rollers and non-drive pulleys, and α is the incline angle. Siegling's guideline values are μ<sub>T</sub> = 0.33 for a fabric underside on a slider bed and μ<sub>R</sub> = 0.033 for rollers. These are example values: get the actual coefficient for your belt and bed from the belt maker. The <a href="#belt-pull-calculator">belt pull calculator</a> on this page applies the same formula and also estimates motor horsepower.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How do you size a conveyor motor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Drive horsepower = (belt pull in lb × belt speed in FPM) / 33,000. Divide by drive train efficiency to get motor power, apply the design margin your belt or gearmotor supplier specifies, and round up to the next standard motor size. Sparks Belting's slider bed calculator uses a default service factor of 1.50. Check gear reducer service factors in the reducer maker's catalog.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the maximum incline angle for a conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>It depends on belt surface, product, and center of gravity. For plastic modular belts, Forbo's Siegling Prolink guide gives rough guidelines of 3 to 5 degrees for a flat top, 20 to 40 degrees for a friction top, under 60 degrees with straight profiles, and under 90 degrees with bent profiles. Bottles and other tall products tip at lower angles. Test the actual product on a sample of the actual belt before fixing the angle.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How do you calculate conveyor throughput?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Products per minute = belt speed (FPM) / product pitch (feet). Product pitch = product length + gap. For multi-lane conveyors, multiply single-lane throughput by the number of lanes. Sustained output is lower because of stops and jams, so size the conveyor using the line efficiency you measure on your own line.</p>
</div>
